  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nQuasi-zonal flow persists across the Central Plains this afternoon \nwith the mid-level ridge flattened over the Four Corners and \nSouthern Plains. Weak energy moving through the mean flow has kept a \nstream of mid and high clouds over the area today, limiting mixing \nand keeping temperatures several degrees lower than yesterday. Heat \nindices have still reached the 100-105 degree range. A well-mixed \nboundary layer will keep temperatures warm overnight, with lows \naround 80 degrees. \n\nExpecting a rinse and repeat pattern through at least Wednesday with \novernight-morning showers and isolated storms giving way to hot and \nhumid afternoons. Cloud cover could be thick at times and limit the \noverall mixing/heating, but highs will hold in the mid 90s to low \n100s. Even with mixing leading to slightly lower dewpoints, heat \nindex values will reach 100-108 degrees Tuesday afternoon. Cloud \ncover and potential showers lead to some uncertainty in how hot \ntemperatures will reach north of Interstate 70, but confidence is \nhigher in heat indices around 105 degrees along and south of I-70. \nTherefore, a Heat Advisory has been issued for areas along and south \nof I-70. The Heat Advisory may need to be expanded north if cloud \ncover and precipitation appear to be less of a factor in afternoon \ntemperatures. \n\nA weak surface boundary sags into northern Kansas on Wednesday, \nleading to additional uncertainty in how hot temperatures will \nreach. South of the boundary, expect another hot and humid day with \nheat index values again in the 100-108 degree range. Again, cloud \ndebris from morning showers could lead to lower temperatures and \nheat indices, but another Heat Advisory may be needed on Wednesday. \n\nThe aforementioned surface boundary could spark some showers or \nstorms Wednesday afternoon/evening as additional energy ejects \nacross the Plains. If storms develop (currently <10% chance), they \nwill likely be high-based with inverted-V soundings supportive of \ndamaging wind gusts. Periodic chances for showers and storms \ncontinue through the back half the week as the boundary holds across \nthe area. Best chances will be along the nose of the LLJ during the \novernight hours; guidance varies on exact position, but highlight \nfar northern Kansas into Nebraska as the most likely area. With the \nfront lingering across the area, hot and humid conditions will \nremain south of the boundary, potentially leading to 100-105 degree \nheat indices for southern portions of the area through at least \nFriday. A stronger cold front is progged to move through this \nweekend, bringing in slightly cooler air to begin next week.",
